          "pause": true,
          "displayType": "poster",
          "buttonOnMobile": false,
          "label": "<p>After an autoclave cycle, the external autoclave tape has changed colour, but th</p>",
          "x": 30,
          "y": 30,
          "width": 40,
          "height": 25,
          "libraryTitle": "Multiple Choice",
          "action": {
            "library": "H5P.MultiChoice 1.16",
            "params": {
              "question": "<p>After an autoclave cycle, the external autoclave tape has changed colour, but the biological indicator shows spore growth of Geobacillus stearothermophilus. What is the correct course of action?</p>",
              "answers": [
                {
                  "text": "<div>Release the load — the chemical indicator confirms sterility</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>External autoclave tape only confirms exposure to the sterilisation process. Colour change does not confirm sterility and cannot override a failed biological indicator.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Re-run the cycle at higher pressure and release without further testing</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Any re-processed load must be re-tested with all three indicator types before release. Skipping re-testing defeats the purpose of sterility monitoring.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Quarantine the load and investigate the cycle failure</div>",
                  "correct": true,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Correct! Biological indicators directly prove whether resistant spores were killed, making them the gold standard. Spore growth means the cycle failed to achieve sterility — the entire load must be quarantined regardless of chemical indicator results.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Release only instruments that appear visibly clean</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Visual cleanliness has no bearing on sterility. Microorganisms are invisible to the naked eye. If the biological indicator fails, the entire load is considered non-sterile.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                }
              ],
              "overallFeedback": [
                {
                  "from": 0,
                  "to": 50,
                  "feedback": "Remember: three complementary indicators must all pass. Biological indicators containing Geobacillus stearothermophilus spores are the gold standard because they directly prove spore kill. A failed biological indicator means the load is not sterile, regardless of other results."
                },
                {
                  "from": 51,
                  "to": 100,
                  "feedback": "Exactly right. A load is released as sterile only when all three indicators — physical, chemical, and biological — confirm success together."
                }
              ],
              "behaviour": {
                "enableRetry": true,
                "enableSolutionsButton": true,
                "enableCheckButton": true,
