          },
          "pause": true,
          "displayType": "poster",
          "buttonOnMobile": false,
          "label": "<p>During an abdominal procedure, you need to gently handle a loop of bowel without</p>",
          "x": 30,
          "y": 30,
          "width": 40,
          "height": 25,
          "libraryTitle": "Multiple Choice",
          "action": {
            "library": "H5P.MultiChoice 1.16",
            "params": {
              "question": "<p>During an abdominal procedure, you need to gently handle a loop of bowel without causing tissue damage. Which instrument is most appropriate?</p>",
              "answers": [
                {
                  "text": "<div>Allis forceps</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Allis forceps have interlocking teeth designed to grasp tissue that is being excised. They would traumatise the delicate bowel wall.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Toothed dissecting forceps</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Toothed forceps provide a firm grip on tough tissues like skin and fascia. Their teeth would damage the thin-walled bowel.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Babcock forceps</div>",
                  "correct": true,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Correct! Babcock forceps belong to the grasping family and are specifically designed to cradle bowel gently. Their broad, smooth jaws distribute pressure without crushing or perforating the tissue.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Spencer Wells forceps</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Spencer Wells forceps are haemostatic clamps designed to occlude bleeding vessels, not to handle tissue. They would crush and injure bowel.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                }
              ],
              "overallFeedback": [
                {
                  "from": 0,
                  "to": 50,
                  "feedback": "In the grasping family, each instrument matches a tissue type: toothed forceps for skin and fascia, non-toothed for fragile tissue, Babcock for bowel, and Allis for tissue being excised. Haemostatic clamps are a separate family entirely."
                },
                {
                  "from": 51,
                  "to": 100,
                  "feedback": "Correct — knowing which instrument matches each tissue type is fundamental. Babcock for bowel, Allis for tissue being removed, toothed forceps for skin and fascia."
                }
              ],
              "behaviour": {
                "enableRetry": true,
                "enableSolutionsButton": true,
                "enableCheckButton": true,
